  "Number": 30,
  "Question": "If \\(\\log_{10}2=a\\) and \\(\\log_{10}3=b,\\) then \\(\\log_5 12\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{a+1}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2a+b}{a+1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{a+2b}{1+a}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{2a+b}{1-a}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{a+2b}{1-a}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The given logarithms have base \\(10\\), so changing to that base lets us use \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) directly:\n\\[\n\\log_5 12=\\frac{\\log_{10}12}{\\log_{10}5}.\n\\]\nSince \\(12=2^2\\cdot3\\), the product and power rules give\n\\[\n\\log_{10}12=2a+b.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(5=10/2\\), so\n\\[\n\\log_{10}5=1-a.\n\\]\nThis denominator is positive because \\(5>1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\log_5 12=\\frac{2a+b}{1-a},\n\\]\nwhich is choice D.",
